<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Nicene Creed</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/325">325</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/4th-century">4th century</a> · Also known as Niceno-Constantinopolitan Creed, Credo</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Nicene Creed is the original statement of belief of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicene-christianity">Nicene Christianity</a>, adopted at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-council-of-nicaea">First Council of Nicaea</a> in 325 to resolve the Arian controversy over the divinity of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesus">Jesus</a>. According to the traditional view, put forward by the Council of Chalcedon of 451, it was amended in 381 by the First Council of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>; the amended form is known as the Niceno-Constantinopolitan Creed. It is professed by the Eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox</a>, Oriental Orthodox, Catholic, Lutheran, Anglican and many other churches, and is said or sung at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eucharist">Eucharist</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nicene-creed.jpg" alt="Nicene Creed" width="330" height="447" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nicaea_icon.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The creeds, the Apostles’ Creed and the <b>Nicene Creed</b> above all, summarise the faith shared by most Christians.</blockquote><a href="/religion/christianity">Christianity · Beliefs &amp; Practice</a></li><li><blockquote>The theory rests on claims that are unproven, and the hymn draws almost all of its content from the Bible and the <b>Nicene Creed</b>; the Irish musicologist William H. Grattan Flood concluded that its words and music came from a Catholic source for Catholic worship.</blockquote><a href="/songs/o-come-all-ye-faithful">O Come, All Ye Faithful</a></li><li><blockquote>It had condemned Arianism, adopted the <b>Nicene Creed</b> and set rules for dating Easter, shaping Christian doctrine for centuries.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/august-25">25 August in history</a></li><li><blockquote>The First Council of Nicaea, convened by the emperor Constantine I, adopted the original <b>Nicene Creed</b>, whose text ends with anathemas against Arian propositions.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/june-19">19 June in history</a></li><li><blockquote>It confirmed the original <b>Nicene Creed</b> and condemned Nestorius, who preferred to call the Virgin Mary Christotokos over Theotokos.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/june-22">22 June in history</a></li><li><blockquote>It condemned Arianism and produced the first version of the <b>Nicene Creed</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/may-20">20 May in history</a></li></ul></section>
